|> Exercise
    handle=_max_sinx_+_cosx_new_new_ver_

    The graph $y = \cos x + \sin x$ from Exercise 
    >>_four_graphs_ has a maximum value greater than $1$:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_maximum_of_sin_plus_cos.svg

    What is this maximum value, and for which
    value(s) of $x$ is it achieved?
    (Reason geometrically, NOT by calculus.)

    |> Solution

        !! Going back to basics, 
        From basics,
        
        $$\cos x + \sin x$$ 
        
        is the sum
        of an $x$-coordinate and a $y$-coordinate.
        We can represent the coordinates as
        two displacements (changes), one in $x$, 
        one in $y$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_v3_2.svg

        The displacements can be summed geometrically
        by tipping the “$\sin x$” displacement to be
        horizontal on its side:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_v3_3.svg

        For short:
        
        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_without_lines.svg

        This generalizes all over the circle (...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_negative_cos_example_2.svg

        |> Image
            !! src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_negative_cos_example_5.svg
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_negative_cos_example_8.svg

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_negative_cos_example_6.svg

        ...some more examples) and,
        given this description, it seems obvious (or:
        it _is_ obvious) that the value of $x$ that
        maximizes $\cos x + \sin x$ is $x = \eta/2$,
        to make the line that rains down at $-45^\circ$
        tangent to the circle at the top right corner of
        the circle, and for which $\cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_sol1_eta_over_2.svg

        (Individually, we have

        $$
        \,\cos(\eta/2)= \sqrt{0.5} = {1 \over \sqrt{2}},
        $$
        $$
        \sin(\eta/2)=\sqrt{0.5} = {1 \over \sqrt{2}}
        $$

        and when we add those two together we get ${2\over \sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}$.)

        Also,

        __any multiple of $4\eta$ can be added__

        [we don't need to say “or subtracted”
        because “multiple” intimates “integer multiple”,
        which subsumes negative multiples]
        to $x = \eta/2$, resulting in an infinite
        set of points at which the maximum is achieved.

        |> SolutionNote

            A similar minimum of
            $$
            \large
            -\sqrt{2}
            $$
            occurs at $x = -135^\circ$
            and its added multiples of $360^\circ$.

        |> SolutionNote

            Annotating the graph of $x \ra \cos x + \sin x$
            with this new information, in degrees and in radians:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_with_max_min_info.svg

            !! We can also use radians:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_with_max_min_info_radian_version.svg

            (Note that $45^\circ = 0.78539\ldots = \eta/2$
            as a real number, 
            !! there is no technical difference between the two graphs.)
            the two graphs really express the same thing.
            Also...

            !! |> SolutionNote
            !! Note that...

            $$
            \Large
            \begin{aligned}
            \ {\eta\over 2} + 180^\circ
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {\eta\over 2} + 2\eta\\
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {\eta\over 2} + {4\eta\over 2}\\
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {5\eta\over 2}
            \end{aligned}
            $$

            !! ...in case you're not familiar with this kind of
            !! arithmetic.)
            ...in case you haven't seen this kind of
            arithmetic before.)
        